 ## Overview
-This method estimates surface normals for volume rendering by evaluating the depth of a ray and two adjacent rays to construct a local surface plane. It solves the problem of inaccurate or noisy normals derived from finite differences on discrete depth maps by using continuous ray evaluation [S1]. Unlike [2D Gradient Estimation for Shading](10739-2d-gradient-estimation-for-shading.md), which computes gradients from a pre-rendered depth image using pixel indices, this method operates directly on the learned geometric model during ray casting, ensuring normals are consistent with the underlying implicit surface representation [S1].
+This method estimates surface normals by evaluating the depth of a primary ray and two adjacent rays to construct a local surface plane [S1]. It computes normals by evaluating depth at discrete adjacent rays and forming a plane from the resulting 3D points, rather than differentiating a rendered depth map [S1].
 
